  "worked_solution": "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{5}{2}(x-4),\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{25}{4}(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{5}{2}(x-4))}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}\\,(x-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{25}{4}(y+1)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{25}{4}\\,(x-4)^{2} & =-\\frac{25}{4}(y+1) \\\\ {(x-4)}^{2} & =-(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
